From f22602e529dbb5635eaa3780b18f8d07db04427e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 12:18:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] net: bcmgenet: reassemble Rx frames that span descriptors
 or arrive early

With the packet ready threshold at its maximum (previous commit), the
Pi 4 receives frames up to 3840 bytes, which is where the 3824 byte MTU
ceiling comes from. Above that the RDMA starts on a frame before the
UniMAC has finished receiving it, and two things follow:

 - the 64 byte receive status block (RSB) at the head of the buffer,
   which is written when the DMA starts, carries a truncated length and
   no DMA_EOP (observed by Dave Stevenson in raspberrypi/linux #5419:
   RSB 0x08403f80, i.e. 2112 bytes = threshold + RSB, SOP without EOP,
   while re-reading the descriptor's own length/status word once the
   DMA was done returned 0x0fe87f80: full length, SOP and EOP);
 - the producer index can be bumped while the buffer is still being
   written, so bcmgenet_desc_rx() unmaps and replaces a buffer the DMA
   is still using, then drops the frame as "fragmented".

Frames longer than the Rx buffer (2048 bytes) are, in addition, spread
over consecutive descriptors, SOP on the first and EOP on the last,
which the driver has never supported.

Rework the receive path around the descriptor length/status words
rather than the RSB alone:

 - bcmgenet_rx_refill() now clears the descriptor word when it puts a
   buffer on the ring, so a zero unambiguously means "the DMA has not
   finished with this buffer";
 - before touching any buffer, bcmgenet_rx_frame_bds() walks the
   descriptor words from the read pointer to find the SOP..EOP run
   that makes up the next frame (up to 8 descriptors). If the run is
   not complete yet the ring is left alone and NAPI is asked to poll
   again straight away (no interrupt will announce the end of a frame
   whose descriptor was already handed over); a frame that never
   completes within 20ms is dropped and counted as fragmented;
 - the buffers of a complete frame are then taken off the ring, the
   first one loses the RSB and alignment pad as before, the others are
   chained on its frag_list, and the frame goes up as one skb;
 - the RSB is still used for the hardware checksum, but only when it
   carries DMA_EOP, i.e. when it saw the whole frame. Frames it did not
   are handed up with CHECKSUM_NONE;
 - if the descriptor word of a frame is still zero, the RSB is peeked
   in place (dma_sync of the first 64 bytes, no unmap). When it shows
   SOP and EOP the frame was entirely in the RBUF before the DMA began
   and the RSB describes it completely, so it is used to size the
   descriptor run. This keeps everything up to the threshold working
   should the hardware turn out not to write descriptor words back
   while the 64B RSB is enabled (in which case only frames above the
   threshold are lost, as today);
 - a buffer that stays on the ring because it could not be replaced
   has its descriptor word and RSB cleared so it cannot be mistaken
   for a finished frame later.

Two ethtool counters are added per ring to see what happens on real
hardware: rxqN_multi_bd (frames reassembled from more than one
descriptor) and rxqN_early_rdy (frames whose RSB did not carry EOP,
i.e. handed over before their end). rxqN_fragmented_errors now counts
frames given up on.

Compile-tested only (arm64, bcm2711_defconfig, W=1, checkpatch clean);
the behaviour of the RDMA beyond 3840 bytes with 2048 byte buffers has
not been observed on hardware, which is the point of this patch.
